18.1  Which package owns a file

TL;DR
  - Every file a package installs is recorded in the package database: ask it
    who owns a file.
  - dpkg -S on Debian and Ubuntu, rpm -qf on Fedora and openSUSE, pacman -Qo on
    Arch.
  - For a program that isn't installed yet, search the repositories instead (see
    17.2).

      dpkg -S /usr/bin/curl
      rpm -qf /usr/bin/curl
      pacman -Qo /usr/bin/curl

On your machine
  dpkg      2 packages
  Snap      2 snaps
  Flatpak   3 apps

Pitfalls
  - Give the full path: dpkg -S curl matches every path that contains "curl".
    command -v curl gives the path.
  - Files you created, and settings a package generated after install, belong to
    no package.
  - With merged /usr (see 7.1), ask about /usr/bin/… rather than /bin/…: the
    database may only know one of the two.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 18.1 --why

Related
  Commands  dpkg, rpm, pacman
  See also  idk 18.2 Native packages vs Snap and Flatpak
  See also  idk 17.2 ELF and "command not found"
